{"id": "De6Od7QRy5R", "title": "Video by therishikatha", "description": "How many times should you walk around EACH god? Most of us do the same rounds everywhere. The scriptures don't. 🙏\n\nPradakshina, Part 2. One verse of the Narasimha Purana (quoted in the Hari Bhakti Vilasa 8.394) gives every god their own count:\n\nएकां चण्ड्यां रवौ सप्त तिस्रो दद्याद्विनायके ।\nचतस्रः केशवे दद्याच्छिवे त्वर्धप्रदक्षिणाम् ॥\n\n🔱 Devi (Chandi): 1 round\n🐘 Ganesha: 3\n🪷 Vishnu (Keshava): 4\n☀️ Surya: 7, the most of all\n🌙 Shiva: only HALF\n\nWhy half for Shiva? Remember Part 1: the water poured on the Shiva linga flows out through the somasutra, and you never step over it. You walk up to it, turn back, and go the other way (Shiva Rahasya 7.26.80).\n\nSome families and regions follow other counts. This is the verse the old puja manuals quote. Next temple visit, count the rounds with your child, and let them lead.\n\n📚 Our picture books bring the gods' stories to kids ages 3–10. Books 1–3, set of 3 for ₹500 at rishikatha.com (link in bio).\n\nHow many rounds do YOU do? Comment your number! 👇 Save this for your next temple visit, and send it to someone who always does three. Missed Part 1? It's on our page.\n\n#pradakshina #parikrama #hindutemple #templerules #hinduism #sanatandharma #shiva #ganesha #vishnu #durga #suryadev #hindurituals #indianparents #hinduculture #rishikatha", "channel": "therishikatha", "uploader_id": "22569163881", "uploader": "Rishi Katha", "timestamp": 1792915211, "like_count": 159341, "comment_count": 346, "http_headers": {"User-Agent": "Mozilla/5.0 (Windows NT 10.0; Win64; x64) AppleWebKit/537.36 (KHTML, like Gecko) Chrome/146.0.0.0 Safari/537.36", "Accept": "text/html,application/xhtml+xml,application/xml;q=0.9,*/*;q=0.8", "Accept-Language": "en-us,en;q=0.5", "Sec-Fetch-Mode": "navigate", "Referer": "https://www.instagram.com/"}, "formats": [{"format_id": "dash-1147925301528267a", "ext": "m4a", "tbr": 54.049, "asr": 48000, "format_note": "DASH audio", "con